With the end of the year approaching, many families are starting to look back on the past twelve months. It's the perfect moment to ask a question that often gets overlooked: is your governance actually working?

Adopting governance is the easy part. A lot of families put practices in place, and many never follow through on them. A charter gets signed, a meeting schedule gets agreed, and then life gets in the way. But how do you measure whether any of it is effective?

One of the simplest tools is the annual review. Looking back over the past year and asking what was working, what wasn't, and whether the family is having good conversations tells you more than any document on the shelf.

In this clip from our podcast series, Mark Tepsich from UBS and I discuss why the annual review matters and how families can use it to keep governance alive rather than letting it lapse.

Governance isn't something you set up once. It's something you revisit, and the end of the year is a natural moment to do exactly that.

